Yes — you should almost always pull (or fetch+merge/rebase) before pushing, so you don't hit a rejected push if the remote has commits you don't have locally. Here's the updated cheat sheet:

## Git Cheat Sheet: Check → Stash → Pull → Commit & Push

### 1. Check what's in your local repo
```bash
git status                 # see modified/staged/untracked files
git diff                   # see unstaged changes in detail
git log --oneline -5       # see your recent local commits
```

### 2. Stash your local changes
```bash
git stash                          # stash tracked changes
git stash -u                       # also stash untracked files
git stash save "descriptive name"  # stash with a message
git stash list                     # confirm it's stashed
```

### 3. Bring in the latest changes from remote
```bash
git pull                    # fetch + merge remote changes into current branch
git pull --rebase           # fetch + rebase instead of merge (cleaner history)
```

> ⚠️ If you still have uncommitted changes (didn't stash, or stashed but not `pop`ped yet), `git pull` can fail or conflict with them. Make sure your working directory is clean before pulling — that's exactly why stashing first (step 2) matters here.

### 4. Restore your changes, commit, and push
```bash
git stash pop                # restore stashed changes (removes from stash list)
git add .                    # stage everything (or `git add <file>` for specific files)
git commit -m "your message"
git push                     # push to the current branch's upstream
```

### Handling conflicts after `git stash pop`
If your stashed changes conflict with what you just pulled:
```bash
git status                   # see which files have conflicts
# manually resolve conflicts in the marked files
git add <resolved-file>      # mark as resolved
git stash drop                # remove the stash once you've resolved everything (pop leaves it if conflicts occurred)
```

### Bonus: useful checks along the way
```bash
git branch --show-current    # confirm which branch you're on
git status                   # re-check before committing
git log --oneline -3         # confirm the commit landed
git log --oneline origin/<branch>..HEAD   # see commits not yet pushed
```

**Recommended full order:** `status` → `stash` → `pull` → `stash pop` → `add` → `commit` → `push`. This way you always start your commit from the most up-to-date base, minimizing merge conflicts later.

If you want to overwrite whatever's on the remote with your local branch, you use a **force push**. This is destructive to the remote history, so it's worth a clear warning before the commands.

## ⚠️ Force Push — use with caution
This **overwrites the remote branch** with your local one. Any commits that exist on the remote but not locally will be **lost** (unless someone else still has them locally). Never do this on shared branches like `main`/`master` unless you're absolutely sure, and ideally coordinate with your team first.

```bash
git push --force              # force push, overwrites remote
git push --force-with-lease   # safer alternative — see below
```

### Prefer `--force-with-lease` over plain `--force`
```bash
git push --force-with-lease
```
This checks that the remote branch hasn't changed since you last fetched it — so if a teammate pushed something you don't know about, it'll refuse instead of blindly wiping their work. Plain `--force` has no such check.


### 1. Check what's in your local repo
```bash
git status
git diff
git log --oneline -5
```

### 2. Stash local changes (if needed)
```bash
git stash
git stash -u
```

### 3. Restore, commit
```bash
git stash pop
git add .
git commit -m "your message"
```

### 4. Push, ignoring remote state
```bash
git push --force-with-lease   # recommended: safer force push
# or
git push --force              # only if you're certain no one else's work will be lost
```

**When this makes sense:** you rebased/amended local commits and need to overwrite the remote history to match, or you're on a personal/feature branch that no one else is touching. **When it doesn't:** shared branches where someone else might have pushed in the meantime — that's exactly what `--force-with-lease` protects against.
